# Rank 1168 / CVE-2023-30470: Hermes's optimizer inferred a JavaScript
# function's call sites from its IR use-list alone, so a non-strict function
# aliased through `arguments.callee` (or `Function.prototype.caller`) let an
# interprocedural optimization run on an incomplete set of callers and later
# read or write past what it had assumed about the function's actual callers
# (CWE-416, GHSA-prjh-7gr6-423r).
#
# identifyCallsites in lib/Optimizer/Scalar/SimpleCallGraphProvider.cpp walked
# every use of a Function value (F->getUsers()) that was a direct CallInst to
# build the set of call sites an optimization pass could safely specialize
# for. In non-strict mode, JavaScript lets a function reach itself through
# `arguments.callee` (directly or via a helper that returns `arguments`, or
# indirectly through a computed property access) without that reference ever
# showing up as a use of the Function value in Hermes's own IR, so the walk
# silently under-counted the function's real callers and a pass trusting that
# set as exhaustive could misoptimize a call it never saw.
#
# The fixed revision adds `if (!F->isStrictMode()) return false;` at the
# top of identifyCallsites, so a non-strict function's call sites are never
# treated as fully known and no aliasing path through arguments.callee can
# be missed. The rank's language is cpp: Hermes's product is a JavaScript
# engine, but the defect and its fix live entirely in this C++ compiler
# pass; there is no JavaScript-level snippet that carries the defect.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap that is not mechanical. Scanning either revision of
# the real checkout reports nothing at this file, and nothing in the ontology
# reaches this shape: every CWE-416 concept in the corpus states a dataflow
# relation between an explicit release site (PointerFree, a delete/free/close
# call) and a later use of the same value. This bug has neither: the code
# never frees anything, and the eventual memory corruption happens later, in
# JIT-generated code for a call the optimizer never saw, not in this
# function. What distinguishes the vulnerable revision from the fixed one is
# that the vulnerable form treats an IR def-use chain (F->getUsers()) as a
# complete enumeration of a JS function's callers without accounting for the
# language's own aliasing rules (arguments.callee, Function.prototype.caller)
# that let a caller reach a function by a path the IR never records as a use.
# Recognizing that gap requires knowing both Hermes's specific IR classes
# (Function, CallInst, the llvh:: use-list) and JavaScript's non-strict
# aliasing semantics together; there is no library documentation a binding
# could be written from, so any binding narrow enough to fire only here would
# answer to this one file, not to a weakness class, failing the genericity
# test outright. No definition change attempted, and no ENGINE-GAPS.md entry:
# the gap is "an interprocedural optimization pass in this engine trusts an
# incomplete callsite enumeration," which is design work specific to one
# compiler's IR, not a mechanical one-line addition to an existing relation.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1168: identifyCallsites treats every Function use as its full callsite set"
  lang cpp
  file lib/Optimizer/Scalar/SimpleCallGraphProvider.cpp
  reject VYQL-MEM-007
  code
  ```
  static bool identifyCallsites(
      Function *F,
      llvh::DenseSet<CallInst *> &callSites) {
    for (auto *CU : F->getUsers()) {
      if (auto *CI = llvh::dyn_cast<CallInst>(CU)) {
        if (!isDirectCallee(F, CI))
          return false;
        callSites.insert(CI);
      } else {
        return false;
      }
    }
    return true;
  }
  ```

test "rank1168: identifyCallsites bails out for non-strict functions that can alias through arguments.callee"
  lang cpp
  file lib/Optimizer/Scalar/SimpleCallGraphProvider.cpp
  reject VYQL-MEM-007
  code
  ```
  static bool identifyCallsites(
      Function *F,
      llvh::DenseSet<CallInst *> &callSites) {
    // Non-strict functions can be aliased in multiple ways (
    // arguments.callee, Function.prototype.caller etc). Do not try to identify
    // the callsites for those.
    if (!F->isStrictMode()) {
      return false;
    }

    for (auto *CU : F->getUsers()) {
      if (auto *CI = llvh::dyn_cast<CallInst>(CU)) {
        if (!isDirectCallee(F, CI))
          return false;
        callSites.insert(CI);
      } else {
        return false;
      }
    }
    return true;
  }
  ```
